# SYNOPSIS

```usage
idlc [--c-out dir] [--man-out dir]
     [--baseline dir [--update-baseline]] [--with file] ... file ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`idlc` reads Swift files that declare libraries with `@IPCLibrary`: an enum holding structs, enums, unions and protocols, from which the macro generates each protocol's client, handler, server and events, and an encoder for each event, for a message kept rather than sent (a resolved theme in memory, radharc(4)).
It reads the same declarations the macro does, and writes what the C side and the reviewers need.
It runs on the dev machine and is built by SwiftPM (`.build/debug/idlc`).
The messages' layout is in wire(6).

For each library in the <file>s it writes, to the directory given with `--c-out`, a C header `<library>.h`, the library's name in snake case.
For each protocol it writes, to `--man-out`, the protocol's page of the manual, section 5.
With `--baseline` it also checks each protocol against its recorded API in `<dir>/<protocol id>.api`.

: `--c-out` <dir>
  Write the C headers to <dir>.
: `--man-out` <dir>
  Write each protocol's page of the manual, section 5, to <dir>.
: `--baseline` <dir>
  Compare each protocol with its baseline in <dir>.
  A protocol with no baseline file is an error unless `--update-baseline` is given.
: `--update-baseline`
  Record the baseline: write a protocol's file when it has none, or when the change keeps it compatible.
  A change that breaks the recorded API is never recorded.
: `--with` <file>
  Read <file> only so that a protocol may compose its protocols (`protocol Directory: NodeIPC.Node`); nothing is written for it.
  It may be repeated.

A change is compatible when a client built against the recorded API still works.
The baseline notes each protocol's id and version, each type's shape and each method's signature.
These break it: a changed protocol id; a lower version; a type, such as an enum or struct, whose shape changed, including the order of an enum's values or a union's cases; a removed method or one whose signature changed; a composed protocol dropped.
A method added after the recorded version must say `@since(n)` with n above the recorded version, and adding any method requires the protocol's version to go up.
Each problem is printed as `<protocol id>: <problem>`.
`idlc` exits 1 if any protocol breaks its baseline.

# EXAMPLES

Check a library against its baselines, then record a change that is compatible:

```rc
% .build/debug/idlc --c-out out/c --man-out out/man \
    --baseline out/base Lib.swift
% .build/debug/idlc --c-out out/c --man-out out/man \
    --baseline out/base --update-baseline Lib.swift
```

